% ============================================================================
% ecosystem-factor-type — the two kinds of ecosystem factor and what
% actually defines each (adj-facts-stdlib, ENVIRONMENT).
% ============================================================================
% Every ecosystem is shaped by two very different kinds of factor: a
% biotic factor is a living thing, an abiotic factor is a non-living
% thing — and both together are what make one ecosystem different from
% another. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? ecosystem_factor_type(biotic, $D)
%       % a_living_organism_that_shapes_its_environment
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `ecosystem_factor_type(factor, description)` carrying the
% source citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query
% — the engine returns the description WITH its source, on the CPU, with
% zero model calls, and abstains on an ecosystem-related term that is not
% one of these two. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description
% and recall the factor type it names:
%
%     ? ecosystem_factor_type($F, a_non_living_part_of_an_ecosystem_that_shapes_its_environment)
%       % abiotic
%
% The two factor types, as a little truth table:
%
%     factor    description                                                          quote (verbatim)
%     --------  -------------------------------------------------------------------  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     biotic     a_living_organism_that_shapes_its_environment                        "A biotic factor is a living organism that shapes its environment."
%     abiotic    a_non_living_part_of_an_ecosystem_that_shapes_its_environment         "An abiotic factor is a non-living part of an ecosystem that shapes its environment."
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from two sibling National Geographic
% Education resource pages, "Biotic Factors" and "Abiotic Factors",
% each introducing its term under its own page heading with a single
% complete defining sentence. WebFetch-verified before writing (twice
% per page — the second pass pulled the full surrounding paragraph for
% each candidate sentence, confirming it stands alone grammatically and
% that the sentence that follows it supplies examples, not a
% qualification the definition depends on). `trust consensus` — National
% Geographic Education is a reputable secondary reference, the same tier
% `biome-type.adj` (a sibling library, sourced from the SAME publisher's
% "The Five Major Types of Biomes" article) already uses.
%
% Unlike most sibling tables in this directory, biotic and abiotic are
% not two items picked out of a longer enumerable list — together they
% exhaust the two-way classification these sources describe, so there is
% no third "real but excluded" factor type to name. Honest abstention
% still holds for any OTHER ecological term a learner might plausibly
% ask about here: "producer", for instance, is a real and commonly
% taught ecology term, but it names a food-chain ROLE (already grounded
% under its own predicate in `biology/food-chain-roles.adj`), not a
% biotic/abiotic FACTOR TYPE — querying it against this table correctly
% abstains rather than inventing an answer. (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table ecosystem_factor_type {
    columns factor, description

    row (biotic, a_living_organism_that_shapes_its_environment)
    row (abiotic, a_non_living_part_of_an_ecosystem_that_shapes_its_environment)

    source "A biotic factor is a living organism that shapes its environment."
    locator "https://education.nationalgeographic.org/resource/resource-library-biotic-factors/"
    trust consensus
}
